  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "An Individual Retirement Account (IRA) is a tax-advantaged, personal savings account allowing individuals to invest for retirement independently of employer plans. Key types include Traditional (tax-deferred growth) and Roth (tax-free withdrawals), requiring earned income to open. Contributions are limited ($6,500-$7,500 in 2023), and early withdrawals before age 5912 may incur penalties. How an IRA Works Tax Advantages: Traditional IRAs offer potential tax-deductible contributions, with taxes paid upon withdrawal in retirement. Roth IRAs use after-tax dollars, allowing tax-free growth and withdrawals. Investments: Funds in an IRA can be invested in stocks, bonds, ETFs, and mutual funds. Withdrawals: Mandatory distributions from traditional IRAs typically start at age 72. Eligibility Requirements Earned Income: You must have taxable compensation (salary, wages, self-employment income) to contribute. Age: There is no age limit for contributing, but you must have earned income. Income Limits: For Roth IRAs, high-income earners may be limited or prohibited from contributing. How to Open an IRA Choose a Provider: Financial institutions, banks, life insurance companies, or brokerage firms. Select Type: Determine if a Traditional or Roth IRA suits your tax situation. Complete Application: Fill out documentation online or in person. Disadvantages of an IRA Contribution Limits: Lower annual contribution limits compared to 401(k) plans. Early Withdrawal Penalties: A 10% penalty plus taxes usually applies to early withdrawals before age 5912. Income Restrictions: High-income earners may not qualify for deductible traditional IRA contributions or direct Roth contributions. Fees Associated with an IRA Setup/Maintenance Fees: Annual administrative fees, though often waived. Investment Fees: Commission fees, expense ratios for mutual funds, or transaction fees for buying/selling assets. Note: The results also indicate the \"Irish Republican Army\" (IRA) as a 20th/21st-century militant organization, which is a different topic.",
